# PO addendum 2026-07-29T11:40Z — what allocates in rag-service while idle.
# Answers the coordinator's re-scoped question 3. Corrects the attribution in
# BOTH the coordinator brief and my own po_index_provenance_20260729T1126.
# Idempotent (marker-key guarded). One priority raise, no mints, no lane moves.
#
# Usage:
#   NOW=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
#   jq --arg now "$NOW" -f scripts/po-triage-20260729T1140-rag-allocation-mechanism.jq \
#     docs/data/orch/orch-state.json | bash scripts/orch-apply.sh

def addend($lane; $id; $key; $val):
  .task_board[$lane] |= map(
    if (.id? // "") == $id and (has($key) | not)
    then . + { ($key): $val, "updated_at": $now, "updated_by": "po/triage-20260729T1140-allocation" }
    else . end
  );

addend("ready"; "FIX-RAG-SERVICE-CLEAN-EXIT-RESTART-LOOP"; "po_allocation_mechanism_20260729T1140";
  "WHAT ALLOCATES WHILE THE SERVICE IS IDLE — measured 2026-07-29T11:36-11:40Z. This supersedes the ingestion attribution in po_index_provenance_20260729T1126 AND corrects the coordinator brief that prompted it. THE BRIEF UNDERCOUNTED THE WINDOW. It reported the 745.8 -> 756.6 MiB rise as happening on 2 POST /index. The full log for that window (docker logs -t --since 11:15:00Z, ALL non-health lines, only 6 exist) is: 11:13:45.53 POST /index, 11:13:46.27 POST /search, 11:29:50.44 POST /index, 11:29:51.09 POST /index, 11:29:51.81 POST /search, 11:29:52.14 POST /search. So the window contains TWO /search as well, and the entire 10.8 MiB step lands on a 2-second burst at 11:29:50-52Z that is a WRITE-THEN-READ pair, not two writes. The read path was omitted from the attribution and it is the likelier allocator. A REQUEST-PROPORTIONAL LEAK IS REFUTED BY THE ASYMMETRY: ~190 host-origin /index across 10:24-10:55Z produced a NET FALL of 4.1 MiB by 11:19Z (749.9 -> 745.8), while 2 index + 2 search across 15 minutes produced a RISE of 10.8 MiB. A leak charged per request would have killed the container during the 190-call burst. Whatever this is, it does not scale with write volume. THE STRUCTURAL FACT THAT REFRAMES THE WHOLE ROW — THE DATASET IS BIGGER THAN THE MEMORY CAP. Measured on the host bind-mount (read-only, no container touched): data/live/lancedb = 785 MiB total against a 768 MiB cgroup cap. Breakdown of rag_entries.lance: data/ 596 MiB across 3137 fragment files; _indices/ 145 MiB across 745 files; _versions/ 32 MiB / 3091 manifests; _transactions/ 12 MiB / 3091 files. A hybrid /search must read across the FTS/vector indices and fan out over fragments, and cgroup-charged memory (docker stats MemUsage = charged memory minus INACTIVE file cache, so ACTIVE page cache is still counted) therefore climbs toward the cap under any read workload — by construction, not by defect. That also explains the otherwise-odd 94-98% plateau that has held since 2026-07-15: a cgroup whose working set exceeds its limit fills to the limit and the kernel reclaims at the boundary. THE DECISIVE TEST IS NOT RUN AND I DID NOT RUN IT — the one measurement that separates reclaimable page cache from a real anonymous leak is the container cgroup memory.stat split (anon vs file/active_file). If file-backed dominates, the memory is reclaimable, the container is NOT on a death clock, and the ~0.51 MiB/min linear extrapolation to a ceiling is invalid. If anon is what climbs, it is a genuine leak and this row is under-scoped. I did not run it because docker exec into rag-service allocates inside the target cgroup and is exactly how RestartCount reached 22 today; PO has no non-exec grant for the VM cgroup tree. WHOEVER PICKS THIS UP MUST RUN IT FIRST — it decides whether the fix is a leak fix, a cap decision, or a read-amplification fix, and every one of those is a different row. DO NOT ACT ON THE LINEAR EXTRAPOLATION: it assumes a constant rate that is contradicted by the 190-call window falling, and by 5 consecutive samples flat at 756.6 MiB across 11:34:46-11:39:30Z (sampler scripts/audits/container-memory-sampler.sh). PRIORITY UNCHANGED P2 this tick — RestartCount is still 22, unchanged since StartedAt 10:12:01Z, i.e. 0 restarts in the 90 minutes of this whole investigation, and this row sets its own escalation bar at >~5 restarts/day.")

| addend("backlog"; "FIX-RAG-COMPACTION-DISK-AMPLIFICATION"; "po_evidence_and_priority_20260729T1140";
  "PRIORITY P3 -> P2 (PO decision 2026-07-29T11:40Z) ON NEW MEASURED EVIDENCE, and this row is now coupled to the memory problem rather than being a tidy-up. MEASURED on the host bind-mount, read-only: data/live/lancedb totals 785 MiB for a corpus of ~11243 rows (index_size measured 05:20Z on RAG-FTS-BUILD-MEMORY-BOUND). rag_entries.lance/data holds 3137 fragment files totalling 596 MiB — roughly one fragment per 3-4 rows, and each POST /index lands a NEW ~6.7-6.9 KiB fragment (the two newest, 11:29Z, correspond exactly to the two index calls logged at 11:29:50.44 and 11:29:51.09). _versions holds 3091 manifests / 32 MiB and _transactions another 3091 files / 12 MiB, so version metadata alone is 44 MiB and grows one pair per write. _indices is 145 MiB across 745 files. WHY THIS IS NO LONGER COSMETIC: 785 MiB on disk EXCEEDS the 768 MiB memory cap, so no read workload can ever have its working set resident, and every hybrid /search fans out across 3137 fragments plus the index set. Fragment count is therefore a direct multiplier on read cost and on cgroup-charged page cache — the thing that has rag-service pinned at 94-98% of its cap. This row and FIX-RAG-SERVICE-CLEAN-EXIT-RESTART-LOOP are the same failure viewed from two ends: the compaction failure-path counter defect that row fixes is WHY 3137 fragments accumulated, and the fragment count is WHY reads are expensive. Neither row currently says that; both should. NOT RAISED FURTHER THAN P2: the corpus is still growing normally, nothing is broken right now, and the P0/P1 ready lane is already saturated. NOT FOLDED into the CLEAN-EXIT row: that row is a ~1-line counter-reset with a tight test plan and folding a compaction/retention redesign into it would destroy a small shippable fix.")

| addend("backlog"; "FU-RAG-DEPLOY-MEMORY"; "po_dataset_exceeds_cap_20260729T1140";
  "DECISION INPUT for the resident-set trade, measured 2026-07-29T11:40Z, no action requested — but this one materially changes the shape of the trade, so read it before sizing anything. THE ON-DISK DATASET IS ALREADY LARGER THAN THE MEMORY CAP: data/live/lancedb = 785 MiB versus a 768 MiB cgroup limit (rag_entries.lance/data 596 MiB / 3137 fragments, _indices 145 MiB / 745 files, _versions 32 MiB, _transactions 12 MiB). Combined with the fixed ~700 MiB embedder singleton already recorded on this row at po_measured_input_20260729, the cap is being asked to hold a fixed model baseline AND the working set of a corpus that already exceeds the whole limit on its own, while the corpus grows ~103 rows/h and RAG-FTS-BUILD-MEMORY-BOUND is time-gated until it reaches ~56254 rows (~5x today). SO THE QUESTION THIS ROW OWNS IS NOT ONLY how many MiB to grant. A cap chosen to fit today loses the same argument again in weeks, because two of the three consumers grow without bound. The options worth costing are at least: (a) raise the cap against the 16GB host budget this row already owns, (b) shrink the fixed baseline (a model release path — embedder.py has none, which is the finding on FIX-RAG-SERVICE-CLEAN-EXIT-RESTART-LOOP, or a smaller/quantised model), (c) shrink the dataset footprint so the working set is bounded (compaction and version retention — FIX-RAG-COMPACTION-DISK-AMPLIFICATION, raised to P2 this tick on this evidence). NOT ESTABLISHED, and it gates option (a): whether the charged memory is reclaimable file-backed cache or anonymous allocation. Nobody has read the container cgroup memory.stat anon/file split — see the same caveat on the CLEAN-EXIT row. If it is mostly file cache then raising the cap buys throughput, not safety, and the real fix is (b) or (c); if it is anonymous then (a) is genuinely load-bearing. Do not size a cap change before that split is measured. PO is NOT authorising a cap change or a rebuild — user-gated infra.")
